_t("blood_tests_reports", "Medical Tests and Reports",
   ["What does a CBC blood test measure?", "How do I understand a blood test report?", "What is a lipid profile test?", "What does a high WBC count mean?",
    "Do I need to fast before a blood test?", "What is HbA1c test?", "what are normal ranges in blood test", "What does the thyroid test TSH show?",
    "What tests are included in a routine health check?", "how to read my medical report", "cbc test meaning", "my report shows abnormal values what should I do"],
   ["cbc", "blood test", "blood report", "lipid profile", "medical report", "lab report", "test report", "normal range", "haemoglobin test", "urine test", "x-ray", "ecg", "scan", "abnormal report"],
   "A blood test measures substances in a small blood sample. A CBC (complete blood count) measures red cells, white cells, haemoglobin and platelets, which helps check for anaemia, infection and clotting problems.",
   "Reports list your value next to a reference range, but ranges differ between labs and a value slightly outside the range does not always mean disease. A lipid profile measures cholesterol and fats; HbA1c reflects average blood sugar over about 3 months; TSH checks thyroid function. Some tests need fasting, so check the lab's instructions.",
   "Bring all reports to your doctor, who will interpret them together with your symptoms and history. Do not start or stop treatment based only on a report.",
   "Contact the doctor who ordered the test soon if a report is marked abnormal, and urgently if you have severe symptoms such as chest pain, breathlessness or confusion."),
_t("vaccination_info", "Vaccination and Immunization",
   ["Which vaccines does a child need?", "Are vaccines safe?", "What is the vaccination schedule for babies?", "Do adults need booster vaccines?",
    "What are common side effects after a vaccine?", "why is immunization important", "what vaccines are needed during pregnancy", "which vaccines are needed before travel",
    "can I take a vaccine if I have a cold", "what is a booster dose", "vacination schedule", "tetanus shot after an injury"],
   ["vaccine", "vaccines", "vaccination", "vacination", "immunization", "immunisation", "booster", "tetanus shot", "vaccination schedule", "immunization schedule", "jab"],
   "Vaccines train the immune system to fight serious diseases before you meet them. Vaccines used in national programmes are tested for safety and prevent many illnesses and deaths.",
   "Children follow a national immunization schedule from birth. Adults may need boosters (for example tetanus), a yearly flu vaccine in some groups, and extra vaccines in pregnancy, older age, long-term illness or before travel. Mild fever, soreness or tiredness for a day or two is common after vaccines.",
   "Keep the vaccination card, follow the schedule from your paediatrician or health centre and ask them about missed doses.",
   "See a doctor promptly for a high fever, swelling of the face, trouble breathing or a severe rash after a vaccine. Call 112 if it is severe."),
_t("chronic_disease_management", "Chronic Disease Management",
   ["How do I live with a long-term illness?", "How can I manage a chronic condition?", "How can I remember to take my medicines every day?", "what is chronic disease management",
    "How often should I see my doctor if I have a chronic illness?", "tips for living with diabetes or high blood pressure", "what lifestyle changes help chronic diseases", "how do I cope emotionally with a lifelong illness",
    "what are examples of chronic diseases", "how to monitor my condition at home", "chronic ilness management", "how to avoid complications of long term disease"],
   ["chronic disease", "chronic illness", "chronic ilness", "chronic condition", "long-term illness", "long term illness", "lifelong illness", "manage my condition", "long-term condition"],
   "A chronic condition is a health problem that lasts a long time, such as diabetes, high blood pressure, asthma, arthritis or heart disease. Good daily management helps people stay well and avoid complications.",
   "Key steps are taking medicines as prescribed, regular check-ups and tests, healthy food, activity within your limits, no tobacco, sleep, stress care and learning your warning signs. A pill box, phone reminder or daily routine helps with medicines.",
   "Keep a simple record of readings and symptoms, carry a list of your medicines, and ask your care team for a written plan. Support groups and family help can make coping easier.",
   "Contact your doctor if symptoms change, medicines cause problems or you miss several doses. Call 112 for a sudden severe symptom such as chest pain or trouble breathing."),
_t("rehabilitation_recovery", "Rehabilitation and Recovery",
   ["How do I recover after surgery?", "What is physiotherapy and how does it help?", "How long does it take to recover from a fracture?", "exercises to recover after an injury",
    "how to regain strength after illness", "What is rehabilitation?", "how can I recover after a stroke", "when can I return to normal activity after surgery",
    "How do I get back to exercise after being ill?", "tips for faster recovery", "rehab and recovary", "should I see a physiotherapist?"],
   ["rehabilitation", "rehab", "recovery", "recovary", "physiotherapy", "physiotherapist", "physio", "after surgery", "post surgery", "regain strength", "after fracture", "recover after"],
   "Rehabilitation helps you regain strength, movement and independence after an illness, injury or surgery. Recovery speed varies from person to person.",
   "A rehabilitation team may include a Physiotherapist, Occupational Therapist, Speech Therapist and a Rehabilitation doctor. Recovery usually goes best with a plan, steady progress, good nutrition, enough sleep and following wound and activity instructions.",
   "Follow your surgeon's or doctor's instructions, move as advised, increase activity gradually, and do not push through sharp pain. Ask the team about warning signs and when to return to work.",
   "Contact your doctor for increasing pain, redness, swelling or discharge at a wound, fever, a calf that is swollen and painful, or new weakness. Call 112 for chest pain or sudden breathlessness."),
_t("substance_use_support", "Substance Use and Addiction Support",
   ["How can I quit smoking?", "I think I drink too much alcohol", "How do I help someone addicted to drugs?", "What are signs of addiction?",
    "Where can I get help for alcohol addiction?", "what are the effects of smoking on health", "how to stop drinking", "I want to stop using drugs but cannot",
    "is vaping safe", "what is withdrawal and is it dangerous", "adiction help", "How to quit tobacco or gutka?"],
   ["quit smoking", "smoking", "alcohol", "alcoholic", "addiction", "adiction", "drug use", "drugs addiction", "tobacco", "vaping", "withdrawal", "de-addiction", "deaddiction", "stop drinking"],
   "Addiction is a health condition, not a lack of willpower. Many people recover with the right support, and every attempt to cut down or quit is worth making.",
   "Signs may include strong cravings, needing more to feel the same effect, failed attempts to stop, using despite harm, and neglecting work or family. Stopping alcohol or some drugs suddenly can cause dangerous withdrawal such as shaking, seizures or confusion.",
   "Talk to a doctor before stopping heavy alcohol or drug use. Support can include counselling, medicines, support groups and de-addiction centres. For tobacco, set a quit date, avoid triggers and ask about nicotine replacement. Tele-MANAS (14416) also helps with addiction concerns.",
   "See a General Physician or Psychiatrist. Seek emergency care for seizures, confusion, severe shaking, chest pain, an overdose or thoughts of self-harm - call 112."),
_t("sexual_reproductive_health", "Sexual and Reproductive Health",
   ["What are the methods of contraception?", "How can I protect myself from sexually transmitted infections?", "What are symptoms of an STI?", "How can I plan a pregnancy?",
    "what is safe sex", "What causes infertility?", "emergency contraception information", "when should a couple see a doctor for not conceiving",
    "how do condoms prevent infections", "what is HIV and how is it prevented", "contraseption methods", "sexual health checkup"],
   ["contraception", "contraseption", "contraceptive", "birth control", "sti", "std", "sexually transmitted", "safe sex", "condom", "condoms", "infertility", "hiv", "sexual health", "family planning", "emergency contraception"],
   "Sexual and reproductive health means being able to make informed, safe choices about sex, contraception and having children. Confidential, non-judgemental care is available.",
   "Condoms protect against both pregnancy and most sexually transmitted infections. Other methods, such as pills, injections, implants and IUDs, prevent pregnancy but not infections. Possible STI signs include unusual discharge, sores, burning on urination or pelvic pain, but many infections have no symptoms. A couple trying to conceive without success for 12 months (6 months if the woman is over 35) should see a doctor.",
   "Use condoms consistently, get tested regularly if you have new or multiple partners, and ask a doctor which contraception suits you. Vaccines such as HPV and hepatitis B help prevent some infections.",
   "See a Gynaecologist, Urologist or General Physician. Seek care soon after unprotected sex if you may need emergency contraception or HIV prevention treatment, and urgently for severe pelvic pain or heavy bleeding."),
_t("disability_accessibility", "Disability and Accessibility-Related Health",
   ["How can people with disabilities access healthcare?", "How do I care for a family member with a disability?", "How can wheelchair users prevent pressure sores?", "what support is available for hearing or vision impairment",
    "What are accessibility needs in a hospital?", "how can I support a person with autism", "health tips for people with limited mobility", "What therapies help children with developmental delay?",
    "how do I find disability services?", "caring for someone with cerebral palsy", "disabilty health care", "What is assistive technology?"],
   ["disability", "disabilty", "disabled", "wheelchair", "accessibility", "accessible", "hearing impairment", "visual impairment", "autism", "cerebral palsy", "developmental delay", "assistive", "mobility problems", "special needs", "caregiver"],
   "People with disabilities have the same right to good healthcare. Health needs can include everyday care, preventing secondary problems and getting accessible services.",
   "Helpful steps include regular check-ups, vaccines and screening, skin checks and pressure relief for wheelchair users, therapy and assistive devices, and clear communication aids. Caregivers also need rest and support.",
   "Ask hospitals and clinics in advance about ramps, interpreters, large print or other adjustments, bring a support person if you wish, and ask your doctor to refer you to therapy and disability services. In India, disability certificates and schemes are available through government disability offices.",
   "Contact a doctor for new skin sores, repeated infections, sudden change in function, pain or distress. Rehabilitation (PM&R) doctors and therapists can plan long-term support."),
_t("emergency_warning_signs_general", "Emergency Warning Signs",
   ["What are the warning signs of a medical emergency?", "When should I go to the emergency room?", "Which symptoms need an ambulance?", "What are signs that someone needs urgent medical help?",
    "when is it an emergency", "how do I know if my symptoms are serious", "red flag symptoms that need a doctor immediately", "which symptoms should never be ignored",
    "when should I call 112", "danger signs in illness", "emergancy warning signs", "what are the signs of a heart attack, stroke or severe infection?"],
   ["warning signs", "emergency room", "emergancy", "red flag", "danger signs", "when to call", "call an ambulance", "need an ambulance", "serious symptoms", "never be ignored", "when is it an emergency", "go to the hospital"],
   "Some symptoms need emergency care straight away. If you are unsure, it is safer to seek help early.",
   "Call 112 or go to the nearest Emergency Department for: severe difficulty breathing; sudden chest pain or pressure; signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech); loss of consciousness or a seizure; severe bleeding; severe allergic reaction with swelling or trouble breathing; suspected poisoning or overdose; sudden severe headache; confusion; very high fever with stiff neck; severe injury; or any thought of harming yourself.",
   "Do not drive yourself if you feel faint or have chest pain. Stay with the person, keep them still and calm, and give the emergency team clear details such as age, symptoms and medicines.",
   "If you think it could be an emergency, treat it as one and call 112 now. Do not wait for an online answer.", urgent=True),
